// Leaf of the let-annotation multimodule probe.
//
// Checked ALONE this file reports 3 errors: E001 for `var n: ST = 7` (an integer
// bound to a struct annotation) and two E004 for the arithmetic on it. Checked as
// an IMPORTED module it reports none of them, because the type annotation on a
// let/var statement does not survive the multimodule path — the binding takes the
// initializer's type instead, and when there is no initializer it takes unit.
//
// That second case is the one with consequences in the real tree: `var out: T`
// with no initializer is how several `*_new` constructors in elf/encode/reloc are
// written, and every field access on such a binding is reported as
// "no field <x> on type ()".

// ------------------------------------------------------------------
// WHERE IT IS NOT, established by measurement rather than by reading:
//
//   * Not specific to imported modules. A `var q: ST = 7` written in the MAIN
//     file of a two-module program loses its annotation exactly the same way.
//     The loss is a property of the multimodule check path, not of being
//     imported.
//   * Not specific to `var`. `let m: ST = 7` behaves identically.
//   * Not the bodies going unchecked. E012 still fires from inside this file,
//     so statements are walked; only the annotation is absent.
//   * Not the parser. parse_optional_type returns Some(Box::new(ty)) and every
//     StmtLet/StmtVar construction in parser/stmts.sio passes it through as
//     `ty: ty_opt` (the two that pass None are destructuring sub-bindings).
//   * Not the statement checkers. All four — checker_check_let_stmt_inplace,
//     checker_check_var_stmt_inplace, check_let_stmt, check_var_stmt — set
//     bind_ty = ann_ty whenever ann_ty is not TyUnknown.
//   * Not the checker entry point. check_items_verdict_boot4 is literally
//     check_items_verdict_boot4_with_module_path(items, empty_path()); the
//     single-module and specialized-multimodule paths run the same function.
//
// WHERE IT MUST BE. run_check_mode forks on closure size (compiler/main.sio):
// one node goes to module_frontend_check_items_with_source_context and keeps
// the annotation; more than one goes to either the merged branch
// (spec_append_item_lists -> specialize_generics -> check_items_verdict_boot4)
// or, when no generic was instantiated, to
// check_modules_verdict_boot4_with_visibility. Both lose it, and they share no
// item surgery, so there are two suspects and one each:
//
//   merged branch : specialize_generics rebuilding statements without `ty`
//   plain branch  : checker_check_items_mut, a different walker from the
//                   ..._store_return_had_error_mut one the single path uses
//
// Forcing the merged branch with a turbofish (SOUNIO_MM_SPEC_TRACE=1 confirms
// "specialized-typecheck over merged 2") still loses the annotation, so the
// merged branch is confirmed affected; the plain branch is confirmed by the
// no-generics form of this very probe.

pub struct ST {
    len: i64,
    last: i64,
}

// Loses its annotation when imported: `n` becomes i64, so `n + 1` type-checks.
pub fn probe_arith_on_struct_ann() -> i64 {
    var n: ST = 7
    n + 1
}

// The shape that matters. No initializer, so with the annotation gone the
// binding is unit and both field accesses fail with E012.
pub fn probe_uninit_annotated() -> ST {
    var out: ST
    out.len = 0
    out.last = 0
    out
}
